@marks7485 
_That's just another way of saying running out. If it stops being viable to extract and use them we have effectively "run out"_
That's a defensible position which I can respect.  The problem is that it does not match the thinking of most people.  Most people with whom I have discussed this with over the last 50 years honestly have assumed that we _will_ pump it all out, and that _that_ constitutes "running out".  And the difference is important.  Here's why:  Back in the '70s, oil companies stopped pumping particular oil fields, and panic ensued because this demonstrated to many people (including, by all appearances, President Carter) that these wells had run dry.  Then there was a lot of deregulation of the petroleum industry (and even more, the natural gas industry) and so thousands of wells were reactivated.  The popular reaction was *THE OIL COMPANIES LIED TO US* because they told us the wells were dry, but now that they can get higher prices with deregulation, they're reopening so that they can rip off the people with higher prices.  There were very few people in the street who understood that the amount extracted was tied to prices, they felt it was purely a matter of technology, and if the technology to extract more exists, then why on earth won't you keep extracting?
